      "content": "You'll typically see affluent, educated, and professional individuals, often with higher incomes and a more desirable demographic profile, moving into the area. These newcomers tend to be white, urban, and middle-class, with a strong desire for convenience, safety, and a sense of community. They're often drawn to the area's perceived gentrification by the promise of a \"revitalized\" neighborhood with improved amenities and a more desirable quality of life.\n\nOn the other hand, the long-time residents who tend to leave the area are often low-income, minority, and working-class individuals who can't afford the rising costs of living, gentrification, and displacement. These residents may be people of color, immigrants, or low-income families who have lived in the area for generations. They're often priced out of their own neighborhoods by rising rents, evictions, and displacement to make way for more affluent newcomers.",
